---
import Legal from '../../layouts/Legal.astro';
---

<Legal
  title="OpenAgent.email vs MailSlurp"
  description="MailSlurp is a hosted email and SMS testing platform. openagent.email is open-source, self-hosted email for AI agents: real inboxes on your own domain, free forever."
>
  <h1>OpenAgent.email <span style="color: var(--ink-faint)">vs</span> MailSlurp</h1>
  <p class="updated">Last checked August 9, 2026 · Something outdated? <a href="https://github.com/openagentemail/openagentemail/issues">Open an issue</a></p>

  <p class="lede">
    MailSlurp is a hosted platform for <em>testing</em> email and SMS — disposable inboxes for QA
    and CI. openagent.email solves a different problem that overlaps at the edges: giving AI
    agents real, persistent mailboxes on your own domain, as open-source software you run
    yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>Side by side</h2>
  <table class="compare">
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>openagent.email</th><th>MailSlurp</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>What it is</td>
        <td>Open-source software (Apache-2.0) you self-host</td>
        <td>Hosted email/SMS testing platform</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Built for</td>
        <td>AI agents receiving real mail in production — signups, OTPs, verification links</td>
        <td>QA teams testing their own app's email/SMS flows in CI</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Where your mail lives</td>
        <td>On your own server</td>
        <td>On their infrastructure</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Cost model</td>
        <td>Free software + your own VPS (~$5/mo covers everything)</td>
        <td>Metered subscription — inboxes and volume are billed</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Inboxes</td>
        <td>Unlimited, persistent, on your own domain</td>
        <td>Metered, typically disposable, on their domains</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>SMS</td>
        <td>No</td>
        <td>Yes</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Interfaces</td>
        <td>REST API, MCP server, built-in human dashboard</td>
        <td>REST API, language SDKs, web dashboard</td>
      </tr>
      {/* 我方 #22 / #19：tasks + phone push；对手侧未核实则写 —，不猜 */}
      <tr>
        <td>Agent-to-agent tasks</td>
        <td>Email-backed task threads with server-stamped states; receiving agent woken; dashboard work-order view; A2A-vocabulary Agent Card</td>
        <td>—</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Phone push</td>
        <td>Per-identity ntfy push with three content tiers (interrupt / subject+from / body+OTP; admin-set)</td>
        <td>—</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Choose MailSlurp if</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You're testing your own application's email or SMS flows in CI/QA.</li>
    <li>You need SMS testing — that's a real differentiator they have and we don't.</li>
    <li>You want throwaway inboxes with zero infrastructure to run.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Choose openagent.email if</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your AI agents need real, persistent mailboxes in production — to sign up for services and receive their verification mail.</li>
    <li>You want every agent on your own domain, at unlimited scale, for a fixed near-zero cost.</li>
    <li>You want agent-to-agent task handoff and phone push on mail arrival, with per-identity control over how much content leaves the box.</li>
    <li>You'd rather own the whole stack than rent inboxes by the thousand.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="cta-row">
    <a class="btn btn-gold" href="https://github.com/openagentemail/openagentemail">Star on GitHub</a>
    <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/docs/quickstart/">10-minute quickstart</a>
  </div>
</Legal>

<style>
  .compare {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 1rem 0 0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }
  .compare th,
  .compare td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.65rem 0.8rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    vertical-align: top;
    color: var(--ink-dim);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .compare th {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
  .compare td:first-child {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 700;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.8rem;
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
</style>
